---@field id string The unique identifier of the Event.
---@field payload? vercel.user.UserEventPayload
---@field principal? vercel.user.UserEventPrincipal
---@field principalId string The ID of the principal who generated the event. The principal is typically a user, but it could also be an app, an integration, etc. The principal may have delegated its authority to an acting party, and so {@link viaIds} should be checked as well.
---@field requestId? string
---@field sessionId? string The ID of the session that the principal's token belongs to, when it belongs to one.
---@field summary? string Compact activity-feed text, when the event has additional details.
---@field text string The human-readable text of the Event.
---@field tokenId? string The public ID of the token that the principal authenticated with, when the request behind this event carried one.
---@field type? vercel.user.UserEventType The type of the event.
---@field user? vercel.user.UserEventUser Metadata for {@link userId}.
---@field userId? string When the principal who generated the event is a user, this is their ID; otherwise, it is empty.
---@field via? vercel.user.UserEventViaList Metadata for {@link viaIds}.
---@field viaIds? vercel.user.UserEventViaIdsList If the principal delegated its authority (for example, a user delegating to an app), then this array contains the ID of the current actor. For example, if `principalId` is "user123" and `viaIds` is `["app456"]`, we can say the event was triggered by - "app456 on behalf of user123", or - "user123 via app4556". Both are equivalent. Arbitrarily long chains of delegation can be represented. For example, if `principalId` is "user123" and `viaIds` is `["service1", "service2"]`, we can say the event was triggered by "user123 via service1 via service2".

